# Paths rsync must never touch on production.
# Leading "/" anchors a pattern to the deploy root; a pattern without a slash
# matches that basename at any depth.
#
# IMPORTANT: every pattern here means BOTH "do not send" and "do not delete".
# rsync's --delete leaves excluded paths alone. So this file protects things on
# the server, it never cleans anything up. Files already on production that
# should not be there are a one-time manual removal.
#
# Anything NOT listed here is deployment-managed: overwritten from the build,
# and deleted from the server if the build does not contain it.
#
# Keep section 1 as short as the app allows. Everything in it is a file no
# deploy will ever overwrite or remove, which is exactly the property an
# attacker wants from a foothold. Managed paths self-heal on the next push.
#
# Note on the *_example.php templates: they are tracked, but the broad patterns
# below also match them, so production keeps whatever copy it already has.
# Harmless, they are references rather than runtime files.

# --- 1. server-owned, must survive ---------------------------------------
# Database credentials, gitignored, server only.
/config/db.php
/config/db_*.php
# Generated card images: rebuilt at runtime, expensive to lose.
/public/img/card_cache/
# Runtime scratch.
/var/

# --- 2. dev-only, do not ship --------------------------------------------
# On production now only because "git pull" shipped every tracked file.
# Excluding them stops further sends but does NOT remove what is already there;
# that is the one-time cleanup listed in the deployment tracker.
/.git/
/.github/
/.gitignore
/.deployignore
/.ddev/
/.idea/
/.claude/
/Readme.md
# Composer runs in the CI runner now.
/composer.json
/composer.lock
